{"id":727,"date":"2020-11-03T11:14:47","date_gmt":"2020-11-03T19:14:47","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=727"},"modified":"2024-08-30T09:48:28","modified_gmt":"2024-08-30T16:48:28","slug":"keyboard-remote-control-2","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/keyboard-remote-control-2\/","title":{"rendered":"3C: Keyboard Remote Control"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140f6ba2d\" name=\"6a22140f6ba2d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting Started<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Make sure that your CoDrone Mini is paired before starting the lesson! If you forgot how to do this, make sure to review how to pair to Blockly <a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/cdmb-pairing\/\">here<\/a>.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140f6bbd4\" name=\"6a22140f6bbd4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Keyboard input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You may be wondering how to use the keyboard to control CoDrone Mini. In the first activity, you will learn how to use your spacebar to turn on your CoDrone Mini LED. First, set the CoDrone Mini LED to off at the start by dragging the turn_off_LED() block to your workspace.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;728&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140f6bc6c\" name=\"6a22140f6bc6c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>While loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Since you want your program to constantly check if you are pressing a key, you will need to implement a forever loop. For this example, use a repeat loop block and connect an is_code_running() block from the orange status checkers menu.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;729&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140f6bcd1\" name=\"6a22140f6bcd1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Conditional<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now it&#8217;s time to check for keyboard input! Remember that when a program needs to make a decision you will use a conditional statement. Go to the logic menu and grab an if block. In the keyboard menu, grab a when backspace key press block. From the drop-down menu, select spacebar.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;732&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If the spacebar is pressed, you want your CoDrone LED to turn on.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;733&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;734&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Else, turn them off by using the turn off block from the lights menu.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;735&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140f6bd2f\" name=\"6a22140f6bd2f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Wait block<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You will need to add a wait block after the conditional statement. This is because if you press and hold the spacebar, you will be sending multiple messages to CoDrone in a very short time, which could be too much for it to process. A delay of 50 milliseconds will be enough to prevent CoDrone from disconnecting.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;736&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140f6bd7e\" name=\"6a22140f6bd7e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>More keys<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you want to check for multiple key presses, you will need to include an else if statement for each key.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;737&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;738&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140f6bea4\" name=\"6a22140f6bea4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding flight commands<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that you have the basic idea, instead of changing LEDs, you will be adding flight commands! First, you will set up the structure of the program to handle roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. Since there are positive and negative values for each flight direction, there are 8 total possible commands plus the spacebar for emergency stop.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this example, the following keyboard commands will be used, but you can change the values to whatever you want.<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><b>W\/S-<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> Positive\/Negative throttle<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><b>D\/A-<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> Positive\/Negative Yaw<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><b>Up\/Down-<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> Positive\/Negative Pitch<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><b>Right\/Left-<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> Positive\/Negative<\/span><\/li>\n<li><b>Space- <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Emergency stop<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;739&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Think about how you should program your if statements. Remember that if and else-if are useful when you only want to execute one of those commands. However, using one if statement and 7 if-else statements means you can only fly in one direction at a time! To be able to fly in more than one direction you need to group your if statements into roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. For example:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If W is pressed, set throttle to 75<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Else if S is pressed, set throttle to -75<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Else, set throttle to 0.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The else statement is very important. It resets the value of throttle to zero whenever neither key is pressed.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;740&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140f6bf2e\" name=\"6a22140f6bf2e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge:<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> Obstacle Course<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that you have your new flight controller, build an obstacle course and try to fly to the finish line without hitting anything! If you need to, adjust the values of the delays and add more keys for more functionality.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Getting Started [vc_column_text]Make sure that your CoDrone Mini is paired before starting the lesson! If you forgot how to do this, make sure to review how to&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/keyboard-remote-control-2\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1671,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":true,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":407,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1814,1760,1817,1763,1777,1737,1815,1793,626,1533,1438,853,144,167,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2108,2125,2121,2122,2111,2126],"class_list":["post-727","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-keyboard","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-remote-control","lesson-tag-senior","module-senior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/727","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":7,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/727\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":8889,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/727\/